        <div id="what-organic-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What the USDA Organic Seal Actually Means on a Protein Tub
          </h2>

          <p>
            The green-and-white USDA Organic seal is a legal claim, not a marketing adjective. To use it, a product must contain at least 95% certified organic ingredients, excluding water and salt, and every farm and processor in the chain must be inspected by a certifying agent accredited under the USDA National Organic Program. A product with 70 to 94% organic ingredients can say <em>made with organic</em> on the front, but it cannot display the seal. That distinction matters on protein tubs, because a few brands lean on the word organic in the name while the label tells a looser story.
          </p>

          <p>
            For plant proteins, organic means the peas, rice, pumpkin seeds or hemp were grown without most synthetic pesticides and fertilisers, without genetic engineering, and without sewage sludge or irradiation. For whey, the rules reach back to the cow. Organic dairy animals must graze on pasture for at least 120 days a year, get at least 30% of their dry-matter intake from pasture during the grazing season, eat certified organic feed, and never receive growth hormones or antibiotics. A cow treated with antibiotics for illness has to leave the organic herd.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why organic whey is rarer and more expensive than organic pea protein. Whey is a by-product of cheese making, so an organic whey powder needs an organic dairy supply, an organic cheese plant and an organic whey processor, all certified. Very few brands have that chain. Organic plant protein, by contrast, is widely available because organic peas and rice are commodity crops with established supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            The seal also covers the minor ingredients. Sweeteners, flavors and thickeners in a USDA Organic powder must be organic or appear on the National List of allowed non-organic substances. So an organic powder sweetened with stevia uses organic stevia extract, and you will not find sucralose or acesulfame potassium in it. If a tub calls itself organic and lists sucralose, it does not carry the full seal, whatever the front panel suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more practical point: the certifier must be named on the label, usually in small print near the ingredient list, such as CCOF, QAI or Oregon Tilth. If you cannot find a certifier on a product claiming to be organic, treat the claim as unverified until you have checked it in the USDA database, which we explain in the last section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-organic-misses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Organic Does Not Promise: Heavy Metals, Protein Quality and Doping Risk
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest misunderstanding about organic protein powder is that organic means clean in every sense. It does not. Heavy metals such as lead, cadmium and arsenic occur naturally in soil, and plants take them up whether the field is organic or not. Rice protein and some seed proteins are especially prone to it. Independent testing groups have repeatedly found measurable heavy metals in organic plant powders, sometimes at higher levels than in conventional whey, because the plant source matters far more than the farming method.
          </p>

          <p>
            If heavy metals are your real concern, the seal to look for is a contaminant test, not an organic one. Clean Label Project, NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice each test finished products, and some brands publish batch certificates of analysis. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">guide to protein powder safety</a> explains what the testing programmes cover and where the limits sit, and our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> roundup ranks products by what they can prove rather than what they claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Organic also says nothing about how much protein you get. Two organic powders can differ by ten grams per serving, and some organic blends pad the scoop with greens, fiber and coconut milk powder that add calories but not protein. The nutrition panel, not the seal, tells you whether you are getting 15 g or 30 g for your money, and how many calories each gram costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, organic certification is not a banned-substance test. A competitive athlete subject to drug testing needs NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which screen for prohibited stimulants and anabolic agents. Only one product in this guide carries a sport certification, and we flag it clearly. Everyone else can treat sport certification as a bonus rather than a requirement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
