        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Ranked These Organic Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the products buyers actually find when they search for organic protein powder on Amazon and at major grocers, then cut any product whose organic status we could not confirm from the label, the brand, or a retailer listing showing the seal. Products had to be currently sold in the US. We did not taste or lab-test anything ourselves; every figure here comes from labels, brand statements, certification bodies and published research.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per serving and protein per calorie came first. A powder that gives 30 g of protein in 170 calories does a different job from one that gives 15 g in 110, and both are legitimate, but you should know which you are buying. We also looked at the protein source, because pea, hemp, rice and whey differ in leucine content and digestibility, which affects how much you need per shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next came independent testing. A sport certification or published contaminant testing moved a product up, because it is the only thing that addresses the heavy-metal question organic certification leaves open. Sweetener choice mattered too: we noted which products use erythritol, which can cause digestive upset in some people, and which use added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            Last, we estimated cost per 25 g of protein using typical online prices at the time of writing. Prices move constantly, with Subscribe and Save discounts, warehouse club deals and seasonal sales, so our figures are deliberately rounded ranges. Use them to compare products against each other, not as a quote.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Organic is a farming standard. Testing is a purity standard.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you want both, buy a product with the USDA seal and a third-party contaminant or sport certification, or one whose brand publishes batch test results you can read.</p>
          </div>
